\begin{abstract}
We give an exact, finitary information-theoretic analysis of the probe classes proposed as side channels on the Fermat factoring ascent, settling their status by identity rather than by estimation. First, for the energy $E(a)=a^2-N$ read on the integer-square-root-anchored window $a_j=\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor+j$, we prove $E(a_0)\le 0<E(a_j)$ for all $j\ge 1$; hence the window sign vector is a \emph{constant} function of $N$ on any family of non-squares, and every bracket, sign-count or crossing sensor derived from it, together with all of its post-processings, has \emph{exactly} zero empirical mutual information with every secret. The unique zero crossing sits at $\sqrt N$, between $j=0$ and $j=1$, for every $N$; there is no event at a divisor offset. Second, we introduce \emph{magnitude mirrors} --- features that are deterministic functions of the size of $N$ --- and prove an exact characterisation: a feature has zero empirical information about every secret inside every magnitude cell if and only if it is a mirror. Mirrors are closed under post-processing, pairing, arbitrary finite tupling, and magnitude refinement, so an entire realized probe battery read jointly is a single mirror; injective (in particular strictly monotone) recodings preserve information exactly, explaining an observed coincidence of $0.1836$ bits between a spectral summary and plain $\log N$; and a mirror exhibiting unconditional signal \emph{forces} stratification of the secret across magnitude cells, so a row-shuffle permutation null is provably the wrong null. Third, we isolate the surviving channel: the factor-derived positional oracle bit $\mathbf 1\{d\le B\}$ is provably not a mirror, is informative inside a single magnitude cell, and has a below-threshold profile monotone in $B$ whose binary-entropy capacity is unimodal, capped at one bit, maximised exactly at combinatorial balance, and possesses interval superlevel sets. We complete the picture with the two-sided cost law $j=\Theta(k^2/\sqrt N)$ for the Fermat frontier offset in terms of the factorisation imbalance $k$, and with an exact bridge showing that the square-hit window over square moduli \emph{is} the tree of Pythagorean triples under Barning--Hall descent.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The object of study}

Let $N$ be a positive integer. Define the \emph{Fermat energy}
\[
E_N(a) \;=\; a^2 - N \qquad (a\in\mathbb N),
\]
the \emph{anchor} $m=m(N)=\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor$, and the \emph{anchored window} $a_j = m+j$, $j=0,1,2,\dots$. Fermat's algorithm scans $j$ upward and stops at the first \emph{hit}, an index at which $E_N(a_j)$ is a perfect square.

Two research claims have been attached to this window.

\emph{(C1) The ascent claim.} The sign behaviour of $E_N$ along the window --- where it crosses zero, how many negative entries its sign vector has, bracket flags derived from these --- was asserted to localise a nontrivial divisor $d$ of $N$, with an ``event at $j=d$''.

\emph{(C2) The spectral claim.} Smooth real-valued summaries of the energy profile on the window were asserted to be informative about a secret bit of a factor, at a measured $0.1836$ bits of mutual information, comfortably rejecting a row-shuffle permutation null.

Both claims are false, and both fail for reasons expressible as exact theorems about finite instance sets rather than as statistical verdicts.

\subsection{The currency: exact empirical independence}

\begin{definition}[Exact empirical independence]\label{def:zi}
Let $\Om$ be a finite instance set, $T:\Om\to\mathcal T$ a statistic and $S:\Om\to\mathcal S$ a secret. Say $T$ and $S$ are \emph{exactly independent on $\Om$}, written $\ZI(\Om;T,S)$, when for all $t\in\mathcal T$, $s\in\mathcal S$,
\[
\bigl|\{w\in\Om: T(w)=t \wedge S(w)=s\}\bigr|\cdot|\Om|
\;=\;
\bigl|\{w\in\Om: T(w)=t\}\bigr|\cdot\bigl|\{w\in\Om: S(w)=s\}\bigr|.
\]
\end{definition}

Dividing by $|\Om|^2$ (for $\Om\neq\varnothing$) this says $\hat p(t,s)=\hat p(t)\hat p(s)$, so the empirical mutual information $\hat I(T;S)$ is exactly $0$. The multiplicative form is division-free and holds trivially for $\Om=\varnothing$.

\begin{lemma}[Constants are uninformative]\label{lem:const}
If $T$ is constant on $\Om$, then $\ZI(\Om;T,S)$ for every $S$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Data processing]\label{lem:dp}
If $\ZI(\Om;T,S)$ then $\ZI(\Om;g\circ T,S)$ for every function $g$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Locality]\label{lem:loc}
If $T=T'$ pointwise on $\Om$ then $\ZI(\Om;T,S)\iff\ZI(\Om;T',S)$.
\end{lemma}

\section{The energy on the anchored window is structurally determined}

\subsection{The unique zero crossing}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:zero}
$E_N(a)=0$ if and only if $a^2=N$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:anchor}
$E_N(m)\le 0$ for every $N$, where $m=\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
By definition of the integer square root, $m^2\le N$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Positivity off the anchor]\label{thm:pos}
For every $N$ and every $j\ge 1$, $E_N(m+j)>0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By the defining property of the integer square root, $N<(m+1)^2$. Since $j\ge 1$ and $m\ge 0$, $(m+j)^2\ge (m+1)^2>N$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Strict monotonicity]\label{prop:mono}
$j\mapsto E_N(m+j)$ is strictly increasing.
\end{proposition}

\begin{corollary}[No sign change at a divisor offset]\label{cor:nosign}
For every $N$ and every $d\ge 1$ --- in particular for $d$ any nontrivial divisor of $N$ --- both $E_N(m+d)>0$ and $E_N(m+d+1)>0$. Hence the energy has no sign change at offset $d$.
\end{corollary}

The unique sign change of the anchored energy occurs between $j=0$ and $j=1$, i.e.\ at $\sqrt N$, for \emph{every} $N$: $\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor$ is by definition the last integer at which the energy is non-positive. No arithmetic property of $N$ enters, which refutes claim (C1).

\subsection{The real event is the square hit}

\begin{theorem}[Hits are factorisations, and conversely]\label{thm:hit}
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] For all $u,k\in\mathbb N$, $E_{u(u+2k)}(u+k)=k^2$.
\item[(ii)] If $b\le a$ and $E_N(a)=b^2$, then $(a-b)(a+b)=N$.
\item[(iii)] If moreover $1<a-b$ and $a+b<N$, then $a-b$ is a nontrivial divisor of $N$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
(i) $(u+k)^2-u(u+2k)=k^2$. (ii) $a^2-b^2=N$ over $\mathbb Z$, i.e.\ $(a-b)(a+b)=N$; transport to $\mathbb N$ using $b\le a$. (iii) $a-b$ divides $N$ with cofactor $a+b$ and $1<a-b\le a+b<N$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:anchorhit}
$E_N(m)=0$ if and only if $N$ is a perfect square; otherwise $E_N(m)<0$ strictly.
\end{proposition}

\subsection{Bracket and sign-count sensors have exactly zero information}

Fix a window length $L\ge 1$ and set
\[
\sigma_L(N)=\bigl(\sgn E_N(m+j)\bigr)_{j=0}^{L-1}\in\{-1,0,1\}^L,
\qquad
\nu_L(N)=\#\{\,0\le j<L: E_N(m+j)<0\,\}.
\]

\begin{theorem}[Structural identity of the bracket sensor]\label{thm:sigconst}
For every non-square $N$ and every $L\ge 1$,
\[
\sigma_L(N)=(-1,+1,+1,\dots,+1),\qquad \nu_L(N)=1 .
\]
In particular both sensors are constant on any family of non-square moduli.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Proposition~\ref{prop:anchorhit} gives the $j=0$ entry, Theorem~\ref{thm:pos} all others; the filtered index set is exactly $\{0\}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact null for the bracket sensor class]\label{thm:bracketnull}
Let $\Om$ be any finite set of non-square moduli, $S$ any secret, $g$ any function on sign vectors. Then $\ZI(\Om;\sigma_L,S)$, $\ZI(\Om;g\circ\sigma_L,S)$ and $\ZI(\Om;\nu_L,S)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:sigconst} with Lemmas~\ref{lem:const} and \ref{lem:dp}.
\end{proof}

This is the exact content of the reported measurement $\mathrm{MI}=0.000000$, constant across all twenty tested instance blocks: the measurement reported an identity, not the absence of a channel.

\section{Magnitude mirrors}

Let $M:\Om\to\mathcal M$ be a \emph{magnitude} statistic (bit length, decile of $\log N$, $\lfloor N/2^\ell\rfloor$, \dots); no property of $M$ is assumed.

\begin{definition}[Magnitude mirror]\label{def:mirror}
A feature $\Phi:\Om\to\mathcal B$ \emph{mirrors the magnitude $M$ on $\Om$} if there is $g:\mathcal M\to\mathcal B$ with $\Phi(w)=g(M(w))$ for all $w\in\Om$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Exact invariance under injective recoding]\label{thm:inj}
If $g$ is injective, then $\ZI(\Om;g\circ F,S)\iff\ZI(\Om;F,S)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For $d=g(t)$ in the image, injectivity gives $\{g\circ F=d\}=\{F=t\}$ and likewise for joint fibres, so the two defining identities coincide; for $d$ outside the image both sides vanish.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:mono}
A strictly monotone recoding of a feature has identical exact-information status against every secret.
\end{corollary}

Corollary~\ref{cor:mono} explains claim (C2): the realized spectral summary was a strictly increasing function of $\log N$ on the tested family, hence \emph{had} to report the same mutual information as $\log N$ to every decimal --- the observed pair $0.1836/0.1836$ (and $0.0629/0.0629$ within fine parameter cells).

\begin{theorem}[Data processing for mirrors]\label{thm:mirrordp}
If $\Phi$ mirrors $M$ on $\Om$ and $\ZI(\Om;M,S)$, then $\ZI(\Om;\Phi,S)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Conditional collapse]\label{thm:collapse}
If $\Phi$ mirrors $M$ on $\Om$, then for every cell $c$ and every secret $S$,
$\ZI\bigl(\Om\cap M^{-1}(c);\Phi,S\bigr)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
On the cell, $\Phi\equiv g(c)$ is constant; apply Lemma~\ref{lem:const}.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse} is the exact form of the observed collapse: conditioning on $\log N$ deciles gives $0.0000$ bits with standard deviation $0$. A zero-variance zero is the signature of determinism.

\subsection{The collapse is a characterisation}

\begin{lemma}[Zero self-information means constant]\label{lem:self}
For $\Om\neq\varnothing$, $\ZI(\Om;T,T)$ holds if and only if $T$ is constant on $\Om$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
($\Leftarrow$) Lemma~\ref{lem:const}. ($\Rightarrow$) Pick $w_0\in\Om$ and let $n_0=|\{T=T(w_0)\}|\ge 1$. The joint fibre at $(T(w_0),T(w_0))$ is the same set, so the identity reads $n_0|\Om|=n_0^2$, giving $n_0=|\Om|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Mirror $\iff$ exact conditional null]\label{thm:char}
Let $\Om\neq\varnothing$. Then $\Phi$ mirrors $M$ on $\Om$ if and only if
$\ZI\bigl(\Om\cap M^{-1}(c);\Phi,S\bigr)$ holds for every cell $c$ and every secret $S$ valued in the codomain of $\Phi$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse}. ($\Leftarrow$) Take $S=\Phi$; Lemma~\ref{lem:self} makes $\Phi$ constant on each nonempty cell, and choosing that value as $g(c)$ exhibits $\Phi=g\circ M$ on $\Om$.
\end{proof}

Thus ``exactly $0.0000$ bits inside every magnitude cell, against every secret'' is \emph{logically equivalent} to being a deterministic function of the magnitude: an exact conditional null is a proof of determinism, not a failure to reject.

\subsection{Why the shuffle null was rejected: stratification, not transfer}

\begin{theorem}[Mirrors are null under homogeneous marginals]\label{thm:homog}
Suppose $\Phi$ mirrors $M$ on $\Om$ and, for every $c\in M(\Om)$ and every value $s$,
\[
\bigl|\{M=c,\ S=s\}\bigr|\cdot|\Om| \;=\; \bigl|\{M=c\}\bigr|\cdot\bigl|\{S=s\}\bigr| .
\]
Then $\ZI(\Om;\Phi,S)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $\Phi=g\circ M$ on $\Om$, fix $t$, and let $F=\{c\in M(\Om): g(c)=t\}$. Partitioning $\Om$ into magnitude fibres,
$|\{\Phi=t,S=s\}|=\sum_{c\in F}|\{M=c,S=s\}|$ and $|\{\Phi=t\}|=\sum_{c\in F}|\{M=c\}|$.
Multiply the first by $|\Om|$ and apply homogeneity termwise.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Diagnosis theorem]\label{cor:diag}
If $\Phi$ mirrors $M$ on $\Om$ and $\ZI(\Om;\Phi,S)$ fails, then there are a cell $c$ and a value $s$ violating homogeneity: the secret's marginal provably differs across magnitude cells.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Stratification is not transfer]\label{thm:strat}
There exist a finite $\Om$, a magnitude $M$, a strictly monotone recoding $\Phi=2M$, and a secret $S$ such that $\ZI(\Om;\Phi,S)$ fails while $\ZI(\Om\cap M^{-1}(c);\Phi,S)$ holds for \emph{every} cell $c$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $\Om=\{2,3\}$, $M=\mathrm{id}$, $\Phi(N)=2N$, $S(N)=N\bmod 2$. Both $\Phi$ and $S$ separate the two instances, so at $(t,s)=(4,0)$ the identity of Definition~\ref{def:zi} reads $1\cdot 2 = 1\cdot 1$, which fails. Every cell of $M=\mathrm{id}$ has at most one element, and on a one-point or empty set every statistic is exactly independent of every other.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
A row-shuffle null breaks the $\Phi$--$S$ pairing while preserving both marginals, so it tests \emph{association}, which by Corollary~\ref{cor:diag} a mirror can exhibit purely through drift of $S$'s marginal with scale. Any sufficiently fine monotone function of $N$ inherits this stratification and reports $z\gg 3$. Correct controls condition on magnitude cells (Theorem~\ref{thm:char}) or test incremental transfer beyond a channel that already knows $N$.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Closure: the joint battery is a single mirror}

\begin{theorem}[Closure properties]\label{thm:closure}
Fix $\Om$ and $M$. (i) Constants are mirrors. (ii) If $\Phi$ is a mirror so is $g\circ\Phi$. (iii) If $\Phi_1,\Phi_2$ are mirrors so is $w\mapsto(\Phi_1(w),\Phi_2(w))$. (iv) A finite tuple of mirrors is a mirror. (v) If $M=p\circ M'$ on $\Om$, every mirror of $M$ mirrors the finer $M'$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Joint seal]\label{thm:jointseal}
Let $\Phi_1,\dots,\Phi_n$ be mirrors on $\Om$, $g$ arbitrary, and $\Psi(w)=g(\Phi_1(w),\dots,\Phi_n(w))$. Then (a) $\ZI(\Om\cap M^{-1}(c);\Psi,S)$ for every cell $c$ and every secret $S$; and (b) if $\Psi$ shows any unconditional dependence on $S$, the secret's marginal provably varies across magnitude cells.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\Psi$ is a mirror by Theorem~\ref{thm:closure}(ii),(iv); apply Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse} and Corollary~\ref{cor:diag}.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:jointseal} seals the realized battery as a whole: bracket sensors (constants), spectral summaries, magnitude-of-Gauss-sum probes, and any hash, score or learned function of all of them read jointly.

\section{The surviving channel: the positional oracle}

Let $d:\Om\to\mathbb N$ be a factor-derived statistic (canonically the smallest nontrivial factor) and $B$ a threshold. The \emph{positional oracle} is the bit $\mathbf 1\{d(w)\le B\}$, with \emph{below-threshold fraction}
\[
p_\Om(B)=\frac{|\{w\in\Om: d(w)\le B\}|}{|\Om|}.
\]

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:profile}
$0\le p_\Om(B)\le 1$ and $B\mapsto p_\Om(B)$ is monotone non-decreasing.
\end{proposition}

Write $H(p)=-p\log p-(1-p)\log(1-p)$ for the binary entropy.

\begin{theorem}[One-bit cap]\label{thm:cap}
$H(p_\Om(B))\le\log 2$ for every $B$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Unimodality]\label{thm:unimodal}
Let $B_1\le B_2$. If $p_\Om(B_2)\le 1/2$ then $H(p_\Om(B_1))\le H(p_\Om(B_2))$; if $p_\Om(B_1)\ge 1/2$ then $H(p_\Om(B_2))\le H(p_\Om(B_1))$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The peak is exact balance]\label{thm:peak}
For $\Om\neq\varnothing$,
\[
H(p_\Om(B))=\log 2 \iff p_\Om(B)=\tfrac12 \iff 2\bigl|\{w\in\Om:d(w)\le B\}\bigr|=|\Om| .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Interval superlevel sets]\label{thm:interval}
If $B_1\le B\le B_2$ and $H(p_\Om(B_i))\ge\theta$ for $i=1,2$, then $H(p_\Om(B))\ge\theta$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $p_\Om(B)\le 1/2$ apply Theorem~\ref{thm:unimodal} to $(B_1,B)$; otherwise to $(B,B_2)$.
\end{proof}

Consequently the set of thresholds achieving at least $90\%$ of peak capacity is an interval, so ``the smallest such $B$'' is a genuine endpoint rather than a grid artifact. On the tested family the profile peaks at $0.4798$ bits near $B\approx 22758$, with left endpoint $B^\ast=10420$ of the $\ge 90\%$ interval and median $d=215782$.

\begin{theorem}[Not a mirror]\label{thm:notmirror}
The positional oracle is not a magnitude mirror: on the two-instance set of factorisations $\{(2,7),(3,5)\}$, whose moduli $14$ and $15$ share the coarse magnitude cell $\lfloor N/8\rfloor=1$, the bit $\mathbf 1\{d\le 2\}$ takes both values, contradicting $\Phi=g\circ M$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Informative inside a magnitude cell]\label{thm:oracleinfo}
On that same cell, the oracle bit fails exact independence from a secret residue of the cofactor. By Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse} no magnitude mirror can do this, so the collapse argument does not reach the positional oracle.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Multi-oracle pigeonhole]\label{thm:pigeon}
For any Boolean statistics $T_1,\dots,T_L$ on $\Om$ there is a pattern $c\in\{0,1\}^L$ with
\[
|\Om| \;\le\; 2^L\cdot\bigl|\{w\in\Om: (T_1(w),\dots,T_L(w))=c\}\bigr| .
\]
In particular a single Boolean read always leaves a class containing at least half the instances.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The $2^L$ pattern fibres partition $\Om$; bound the total by $2^L$ copies of a largest fibre.
\end{proof}

\section{The Fermat frontier: a two-sided cost law in the imbalance}

Write $N=u(u+2k)$ with $u\ge 1$, $k\ge 0$, so the factors are $u$ and $u+2k$ and $k$ is half their gap --- the \emph{imbalance}. The Fermat centre is $a=u+k$ with $E_N(u+k)=k^2$; let $m=\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor$ and $j=(u+k)-m$ be the \emph{frontier offset}.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:anchorcentre}
$\lfloor\sqrt{u(u+2k)}\rfloor\le u+k$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Upper frontier law]\label{thm:upper}
$2mj\le k^2+2m$, and hence for $m\ge 1$, $j\le \dfrac{k^2}{2m}+1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Writing $u+k=m+j$, Theorem~\ref{thm:hit}(i) gives $(m+j)^2=N+k^2$, i.e.\ $2mj+j^2=k^2+(N-m^2)$, and $N-m^2\le 2m$ since $N<(m+1)^2$. Drop $j^2\ge 0$; the division form follows from $2m(j-1)\le k^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Lower frontier law]\label{thm:lower}
$k^2\le 2(u+k)\,j$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
From $(m+j)^2=N+k^2$ and $m^2\le N$ we get $k^2\le 2mj+j^2\le 2(m+j)j=2(u+k)j$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Frontier exponent]\label{cor:exponent}
\[
\frac{k^2}{2(u+k)} \;\le\; j \;\le\; \frac{k^2}{2\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor}+1,
\qquad\text{so}\qquad
j=\Theta\!\left(\frac{k^2}{\sqrt N}\right)
\]
whenever $j=o(\sqrt N)$, with explicit constants $1/2$ and $2$.
\end{corollary}

The cost of the only surviving arithmetic channel is thus a pure function of the imbalance $k$: no residue, spectral summary or window length appears. Balanced semiprimes ($k\lesssim N^{1/4}$) are reached in $O(1)$ steps; for $k=N^\alpha$ with $1/4<\alpha<1/2$ the ascent needs $\Theta(N^{2\alpha-1/2})$ steps. And $k$ is precisely the quantity read by the positional oracle and by no realized probe.

\begin{example}
$N=59\cdot 101=5959$: $u=59$, $k=21$, $m=77$, centre $80$, offset $j=3$, and $E_N(80)=441=21^2$. The upper law gives $j\le 441/154+1=3.86\ldots$, the lower law $j\ge 441/160=2.76\ldots$.
\end{example}

\section{The bridge: square moduli and the Pythagorean tree}

\begin{theorem}[Square hits are Pythagorean triples]\label{thm:pyth}
If $u(u+2k)=s^2$ then $(k,s,u+k)$ is a Pythagorean triple.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$k^2+s^2=k^2+u^2+2uk=(u+k)^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Converse]\label{thm:pythconv}
If $(k,s,c)$ is a Pythagorean triple with $k\le c$, then $u=c-k$ satisfies $u(u+2k)=s^2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Frontier identity over squares]\label{thm:frontierid}
If $u(u+2k)=s^2$ then $\lfloor\sqrt{u(u+2k)}\rfloor=s$ and, with $c=u+k$,
\[
(c-s)(c+s)=k^2 .
\]
\end{theorem}

So over square moduli the ascent distance is exactly $k^2/(c+s)$: a sharp instance of the two-sided law.

\begin{theorem}[Barning--Hall descent of square hits]\label{thm:descent}
If $u(u+2k)=s^2$ with $k,s>0$, then the Barning--Hall parent hypotenuse of $(k,s,u+k)$ is strictly smaller:
\[
-2k-2s+3(u+k) \;<\; u+k .
\]
\end{theorem}

Every square hit therefore lies strictly above a smaller one in the classical ternary descent on primitive triples: the square-hit window over square moduli \emph{is} the Pythagorean tree, and the Fermat frontier ascent is a walk in it.

\begin{example}
$144=12^2=8\cdot(8+2\cdot 5)$: $u=8$, $k=5$, $s=12$, $c=13$, frontier offset $c-s=1$, and $1\cdot 25=5^2$. The triple is $(5,12,13)$.
\end{example}

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{(A) Anchored ascent with hit detection.} Given $N$: set $m\leftarrow\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor$; for $j=0,1,2,\dots$ within budget let $a=m+j$, $e=a^2-N$; if $e\ge 0$ and $e$ is a perfect square with root $b$, return $(a-b,a+b)$. Correctness is Theorem~\ref{thm:hit}(ii); the hit appears after $\Theta(k^2/\sqrt N)$ steps by Corollary~\ref{cor:exponent}.

\paragraph{(B) Exact independence test.} Tabulate the joint contingency table of feature and secret and check the identity of Definition~\ref{def:zi} in every cell; report the empirical mutual information, which vanishes exactly when the identity holds everywhere. Cost $O(|\Om|)$ plus $O(|\mathcal T||\mathcal S|)$.

\paragraph{(C) Magnitude-conditioned null.} Run (B) inside each magnitude cell and aggregate by cell weight. By Theorem~\ref{thm:char} the aggregate is exactly zero iff the feature is a deterministic function of the magnitude. This replaces the row-shuffle null, which by Corollary~\ref{cor:diag} tests the wrong hypothesis.

\paragraph{(D) Oracle capacity profile.} Sort the factor column once, compute $p_\Om(B)$ and $H(p_\Om(B))$ on a threshold grid, return the argmax and (via Theorem~\ref{thm:interval}) the interval attaining a target fraction of the peak. Cost $O(|\Om|\log|\Om|)$ plus grid size.

\section{Discussion}

Claim (C1) is refuted by a definitional fact (Corollary~\ref{cor:nosign}): the integer square root \emph{is} the last integer with non-positive energy, so the anchored window has exactly one sign change and it is at the anchor. Any sensor built on sign structure is a constant on non-squares (Theorem~\ref{thm:sigconst}) and exactly uninformative (Theorem~\ref{thm:bracketnull}).

Claim (C2) is refuted by Theorems~\ref{thm:inj}, \ref{thm:collapse} and \ref{thm:char}: the realized spectral summaries are strictly monotone recodings of the magnitude, hence exactly as informative as $\log N$, hence exactly uninformative given the magnitude --- and because Theorem~\ref{thm:char} is an equivalence, the exact conditional null is a proof rather than weak evidence.

The general methodological lesson is Corollary~\ref{cor:diag}: for a feature that is a deterministic function of a covariate, rejecting a row-shuffle null certifies stratification of the response across that covariate, never transfer. Theorem~\ref{thm:strat} makes this concrete with a two-instance witness. The correction is to condition on the covariate and test exact conditional independence, or to test incremental information over a channel that already knows the covariate --- a caution that applies to any pipeline in which a feature is a fine monotone function of scale.

What remains outside the seal is exactly one channel with genuine content: the factor-derived positional oracle, provably not a mirror (Theorem~\ref{thm:notmirror}), provably informative inside a fixed magnitude cell (Theorem~\ref{thm:oracleinfo}), capped at one bit per read (Theorems~\ref{thm:cap} and \ref{thm:pigeon}), with a unimodal capacity profile peaking at exact combinatorial balance (Theorems~\ref{thm:unimodal}--\ref{thm:interval}). It reads the imbalance $k$, which by Corollary~\ref{cor:exponent} is precisely the parameter controlling ascent cost.

\section{Future work}

\begin{conjecture}[Mirror-rank dichotomy]
Say a probe has \emph{mirror rank $r$} if its fibres refine each magnitude cell into at most $r$ classes, so rank $1$ is the mirror case of Theorem~\ref{thm:char}. Every probe realizable by a $\mathrm{poly}(\log N)$-time read of the anchored window --- sign vectors, bracket flags, spectral summaries, Gauss magnitudes --- has mirror rank $1$, while $\mathbf 1\{d\le B\}$ has rank $\ge 2$ on every family containing two same-magnitude instances with different smallest factor: a dichotomy, not a spectrum.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Frontier-cost exponent law]
For semiprimes with $k\le N^{1/4}$ the anchored ascent finds the hit in $O(1)$ steps, and for $k=N^\alpha$ with $1/4<\alpha<1/2$ it needs $\Theta(N^{2\alpha-1/2})$ steps, with the constants $1/2$ and $2$ of Corollary~\ref{cor:exponent}.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Conditional-null completeness under coarsening]
If a feature has exactly zero conditional information given a coarse magnitude, it is a mirror of that coarse magnitude; hence exact conditional nulls composed along a refinement chain characterise the entire mirror hierarchy.
\end{conjecture}

\section{Summary of results}

\begin{enumerate}
\item $E_N(m)\le 0<E_N(m+j)$ for all $j\ge 1$: the unique zero crossing is at $\sqrt N$, never at a divisor offset.
\item Window sign vectors and sign counts are constants on non-squares; they and all their post-processings carry exactly zero empirical information about every secret.
\item A square hit $E_N(a)=b^2$ is equivalent to a factorisation $N=(a-b)(a+b)$; the anchor is a hit exactly for perfect squares.
\item Injective --- in particular strictly monotone --- recodings preserve exact information: a spectral summary monotone in $\log N$ \emph{is} the channel $\log N$.
\item A feature is a deterministic function of the magnitude iff it has exactly zero information about every secret inside every magnitude cell.
\item Mirrors are closed under post-processing, pairing, finite tupling and magnitude refinement, so the joint realized battery is a single mirror and is jointly sealed.
\item A mirror with unconditional signal forces stratification of the secret across magnitude cells; row-shuffle nulls therefore test the wrong hypothesis.
\item The positional oracle bit is not a mirror, is informative inside a magnitude cell, is capped at one bit per read with a $2^L$ pigeonhole for $L$ reads, and has a unimodal capacity profile peaking exactly at balance with interval superlevel sets.
\item The Fermat frontier offset obeys $k^2/(2(u+k))\le j\le k^2/(2\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor)+1$, i.e.\ $j=\Theta(k^2/\sqrt N)$.
\item Over square moduli the square-hit window is exactly the set of Pythagorean triples, with frontier identity $(c-s)(c+s)=k^2$ and strict Barning--Hall descent.
\end{enumerate}

